  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nToday will feature a breezy and cooler west northwest flow over the \narea. 850mb temperatures will be down around +4-5C through the day \nbehind the cold front. This will open the door to some lake effect \nshowers developing, especially as some modest instability forms from \ndaytime heating this afternoon. Some isolated showers could make it \nas far south as the Twin Tiers by late afternoon. Otherwise, \nconditions will be partly sunny north to mostly sunny south into NE \nPA. The west-northwest winds will be breezy, between 8-15 mph with \ngusts between 20-30 mph possible. The wind diminish tonight, but \nscattered lake effect rain showers will continue to be possible \nalong and north of I-90 overnight. It will be seasonably cool with \nlows in the 40s. Heading into Friday, there could be isolated \nlingering lake effect showers up north in the morning, otherwise \nhigh pressure builds from the upper Ohio Valley, bringing drier and \nmainly sunny conditions. Northwest winds continue, a little breezy \nstill. Mild with highs in the 60s for most locations.\n\nIt will be cooler Friday night, with frost up north where the \ngrowing season has already ended. Lows range in the 30s to low 40s \nfor most locations in the forecast area. Patchy fog also possible.\n\nSaturday looks to feature high pressure, sunny weather, with winds \nshifting out of the south up to 10 mph in the afternoon. It will be \nwarmer with highs in the mid-60s to low 70s expected. Overall, a \nfantastic start to the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThere remains a good deal of uncertainty on the exact track and \ntiming of the remnants of Isaias as it heads north-northeast for the \nend of the weekend. The latest ensemble guidance has slowed down a \ntouch, but some of the deterministic guidance is faster bringing the \nrain in. At this time it appears the rain will quickly overspread \nthe area from southwest to northeast during the day on Sunday; with \nperiods of rain continuing into Sunday night, before exiting east by \nMonday morning. Exact rainfall amounts and any thunderstorm \npotential remains uncertain this far out in time. Probabilities\nfor 1\"+ of rainfall are around 25-40% across the region at this\ntime, with a 10% chance for 2\" of rain near the Wyoming valley\nregion of NE PA (lower elsewhere). The mean 24 hour rainfall\namounts are between 0.75 to 1\" from the Twin Tiers south into NE\nPA, with around 0.6 inches further north toward I-90.\n\nPWATs rise up to at least 1.50 inches, but some even deeper \ntropical moisture may be nearby. This will certainly bare \nwatching as we get closer in time.\n\nAfter this system passes through it looks to remain mild Monday and \nTuesday with renewed shower chances entering the forecast. A fairly \nstrong cold front may pass through the area later on Tuesday, \nbringing much cooler, fall air to the area by the middle of next week.",
